---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * Every statement here is checkable against committed source, and the source
 * is linked where it is. Written from: src/layouts/Layout.astro (which
 * analytics load), public/analytics.js (how PostHog is configured),
 * public/_worker.js (what the signup form stores), public/_headers (which
 * third-party hosts the page is allowed to talk to at all).
 *
 * If you change what the site collects, change this page in the same commit.
 */
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '@/components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '@/components/Footer.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import Eyebrow from '@/components/Eyebrow.astro';

const updated = '25 July 2026';
const repo = 'https://github.com/kontourai/kontourai.io/blob/main';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy — what this site collects, in plain terms · Kontour AI"
  description="What kontourai.io collects when you read it, what it stores if you give us your email, which third parties are involved, and how to get your data deleted."
>
  <Nav />

  <Section contour size="hero" borderBottom>
    <div class="container container--readable">
      <Eyebrow accent="gold" gap="sm">Privacy</Eyebrow>
      <h1 class="display-lg privacy-title">What this site collects.</h1>
      <p class="lead measure privacy-lead">
        We sell receipts for a living, so here is one for the site itself. Every
        line below can be checked against the source that implements it — the
        links go to the actual files.
      </p>
      <p class="fine privacy-updated">Last updated {updated} · Kontour AI LLC</p>
      <p class="body measure privacy-lead">
        This page is about the website. The Station app collects different
        things, on hardware you control, and has its own policy:{' '}
        <a href="/privacy/station/">what the Station app collects →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section borderBottom>
    <div class="container container--readable">
      <h2 class="display-md privacy-h2">If you just read the site</h2>
      <p class="body measure">
        No account, no cookies, and nothing you type — because there is nothing
        to type. Three things still happen that you should know about.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Analytics</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        Two analytics tools run in production: <a href="https://umami.is/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Umami</a>{' '}
        and <a href="https://posthog.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostHog</a>.
        They record which pages get visited, which links get clicked, and coarse
        technical detail like browser, operating system, and country. PostHog is
        configured to keep no cookies (it uses your browser's local storage), and
        we never attach a name or email to any of it — the code never calls{' '}
        <code class="code-plain">identify</code>, so events are not tied to a
        person. Both tools honour the "Do Not Track" setting: switch it on and
        neither one loads.{' '}
        <a href={`${repo}/public/analytics.js`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">See the configuration →</a>
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Session recordings</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        PostHog records anonymous playback of site sessions, so we can see where
        a page confuses people. Every input field is masked before anything is
        sent, so the one thing you could type here — your email address in the
        signup box — is never captured in a recording. Recordings are not linked
        to an identity.
      </p>

      <h3 class="privacy-h3">Fonts and hosting</h3>
      <p class="body measure">
        The site's typefaces load from Google Fonts, which means Google receives
        your IP address and browser details when a page loads. The site is hosted
        on Cloudflare Pages, so Cloudflare handles the request and keeps its own
        standard server-side logs. Neither is something we control beyond the
        decision to use them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section borderBottom>
    <div class="container container--readable">
      <h2 class="display-md privacy-h2">If you give us your email</h2>
      <p class="body measure">
        The signup form stores exactly five things, and this is the complete
        list: your email address, which page you submitted it from, the time you
        submitted it, your browser's user-agent string, and the page that
        referred you. That's it — there is no hidden field and no enrichment
        step. It goes into a private Cloudflare key-value store that only we can
        read.{' '}
        <a href={`${repo}/public/_worker.js`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">See the code that writes it →</a>
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        To stop the form being hammered, the same code counts submissions per IP
        address for ten minutes at a time. That counter expires on its own and is
        never written into your signup record.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        We use the address to send occasional build updates, and to reply if you
        started a conversation. We do not sell it, rent it, share it with
        advertisers, or load it into an ad network.
      </p>
      <p class="body measure">
        We keep it until you ask us to delete it. There is no self-serve
        unsubscribe link yet — that is a real gap, not a policy — so for now,
        email <Fragment set:html="<!--email_off-->" /><a href="mailto:hello@kontourai.io">hello@kontourai.io</a><Fragment set:html="<!--email_on-->" /> and ask,
        and we will delete the record and confirm. You can ask for a copy of what
        we hold the same way.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section borderBottom>
    <div class="container container--readable">
      <h2 class="display-md privacy-h2">Everyone we hand data to</h2>
      <p class="body measure">
        This is the full list. The site's security policy blocks the browser from
        contacting any other outside host, so it can't quietly grow — you can
        read the allowlist yourself.{' '}
        <a href={`${repo}/public/_headers`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">See the allowlist →</a>
      </p>
      <ul class="privacy-list">
        <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> — hosting, request logs, and the store holding signup emails.</li>
        <li><strong>Umami</strong> — page and click analytics.</li>
        <li><strong>PostHog</strong> — page and click analytics, plus masked session recordings.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Fonts</strong> — serves the typefaces; receives your IP address as a result.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <div class="container container--readable">
      <h2 class="display-md privacy-h2">Changes, and how to reach us</h2>
      <p class="body measure">
        If what the site collects changes, this page changes in the same commit —
        the history is public, so you can see what it said before.
        Questions, deletions, or anything else:{' '}
        <Fragment set:html="<!--email_off-->" /><a href="mailto:hello@kontourai.io">hello@kontourai.io</a><Fragment set:html="<!--email_on-->" />.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .privacy-title {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .privacy-lead {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  .privacy-updated {
    color: var(--color-chalk-3);
  }
  .privacy-h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .privacy-h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--color-chalk);
    margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .privacy-list {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    max-width: 66ch;
    color: var(--color-chalk-2);
  }
  .privacy-list strong {
    color: var(--color-chalk);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
</style>
